When a checkbox or radio button keeps its native appearance, Ladybird
lays it out with zero padding and border widths, and paints no CSS
background or border under the control. Its computed values stay as the
author set them: Only the used values change.

Blink does the same in layout and painting, but at style time:
LayoutTheme::AdjustCheckboxStyle() and AdjustRadioStyle() call
ResetPadding() and ResetBorder(), so getComputedStyle() reports zero
padding and border too. And BoxFragmentPainter skips PaintBackground()
and PaintBorder() once ThemePainter::Paint() has drawn the control.

WebKit also adjusts the style: RenderTheme::controlPadding() and
controlBorder() return zero for Checkbox and Radio, and RenderTheme’s
style adjustment for checkboxes and radio buttons applies them.
RenderBox::paintBoxDecorations() then paints neither the background nor
the border when theme().paint() draws the control.

Gecko, like Ladybird, keeps the computed values and changes only the
used ones: Theme::GetWidgetPadding() returns zero padding for them (they
“can’t have author-specified padding”), and Theme::GetWidgetBorder()
gives them no border. In nsDisplayList.cpp, a themed frame gets an
nsDisplayThemedBackground instead of its CSS background.
